=== DESIGN ===

D1. Catalog endpoint — GET /signals/catalog
    Response shape (Decision #1):
        {
          "signals": [
            {
              "field":               "BatteryLevel",
              "destination":         "signal_log",
              "value_kind":          "ValueKindFloat",
              "last_seen_at":        "2026-05-06T11:55:32Z",   // null if never observed
              "sample_count_total":  84231,                     // null if never observed
              "vehicle_count":       3                          // null if never observed
            },
            ...
          ],
          "generated_at": "2026-05-06T18:30:00Z"
        }
    Sort: by field ASC for stable rendering across requests.

    Catalog source-of-truth: the embedded routing.yaml parsed via
    router.Load() at handler construction. Every entry (including dest=drop
    and dest=unit_history) is included so the frontend can show the full
    routing surface; the frontend can filter client-side if it only wants
    user-facing signals. value_kind comes from protomodel.SignalsByName
    keyed on the same Field name; routing.yaml entries with no protomodel
    counterpart (none today, but defensively handled) get
    "ValueKindUnknown".

    Aggregate query (per-request):
        SELECT field,
               MAX(ts)                    AS last_seen_at,
               COUNT(*)                   AS sample_count_total,
               COUNT(DISTINCT vehicle_id) AS vehicle_count
        FROM signal_log
        GROUP BY field

    The signal_log_field_ts index covers the GROUP BY but not the
    COUNT(DISTINCT vehicle_id) aggregate; that scan reads the chunk-level
    indexes per partition and is the slowest part of the response. Per
    Decision #6, we accept 1-2s on the hot dataset for an admin endpoint.
    If real production latency exceeds 5s the escape hatch drops the
    counts and returns last_seen_at alone — flagged here, not exercised.

    Merge in Go: iterate the parsed-routing slice, look up aggregates by
    field name in the result map, emit one row per routing entry. This
    keeps the SQL simple (no LEFT JOIN of routing-yaml table that doesn't
    exist) and makes the routed-but-unobserved case (NULL last_seen_at)
    a natural fall-through.

D2. Observations endpoint — GET /signals/observations?...
    Response shape (Decision #3):
        {
          "count":  37,
          "total":  4218,
          "observations": [
            {
              "vehicle_id": 42,
              "ts":         "2026-05-06T11:55:32Z",
              "field":      "BatteryLevel",
              "value_kind": "ValueKindFloat",
              "value":      78.5
            },
            ...
          ]
        }

    Query parameters (Decision #4, all snake_case):
        vehicle_id   optional, comma-separated (e.g. "42,7,99")
        field        optional, comma-separated
        since        optional, RFC3339 (inclusive lower bound on ts)
        until        optional, RFC3339 (inclusive upper bound on ts)
        limit        default 100, max 1000
        offset       default 0

    Filter SQL (built dynamically with positional placeholders):
        WHERE 1=1
          [AND vehicle_id = ANY($N::bigint[])]
          [AND field      = ANY($N::text[])]
          [AND ts >= $N]
          [AND ts <= $N]
        ORDER BY ts DESC, vehicle_id ASC, field ASC
        LIMIT  $N
        OFFSET $N

    `ts DESC` matches the operator's "show me the latest events first"
    expectation that the dropped legacy table satisfied; the
    signal_log_vehicle_field_ts and signal_log_field_ts indexes both
    sort ts DESC so this is index-aligned.

    Total count: a separate SELECT COUNT(*) over the same WHERE clause.
    On a 100M-row signal_log this is the expensive query — for that
    reason `total` is documented as a best-effort cardinality and the
    handler caps the WHERE-less COUNT at LIMIT*10 when no filters are
    supplied (the unfiltered admin probe doesn't need an exact 100M
    answer to render the "Load more?" affordance).

    Decision #5 value rendering — pick the typed column dictated by
    value_kind and emit it as `value`:
        ValueKindString (1) -> str_value
        ValueKindBool   (2) -> bool_value
        ValueKindInt32  (3) -> int_value
        ValueKindInt64  (4) -> int_value
        ValueKindFloat  (5) -> float_value
        ValueKindDouble (6) -> float_value
        ValueKindEnum   (7) -> int_value (proto enum number)
        ValueKindTime   (9) -> time_value (RFC3339Nano)
        anything else       -> null
    The handler stores the populated value in the JSON `value` field;
    the consumer's `value_kind` discriminator says which Go scalar type
    to expect.

D3. Repo interface + handler interface
    repo:
        type SignalsCatalogRepo struct { pool signalsCatalogPool }
        type signalsCatalogPool interface {
            Query(ctx, sql, args ...) (pgx.Rows, error)
            QueryRow(ctx, sql, args ...) pgx.Row
        }
        method:
            CatalogAggregates(ctx) (map[string]CatalogAggregate, error)
            ObservationsCount(ctx, params ObservationsParams) (int64, error)
            Observations(ctx, params ObservationsParams) ([]SignalObservation, error)

    handler interface (for tests):
        type signalsCatalogRepository interface {
            CatalogAggregates(...)
            ObservationsCount(...)
            Observations(...)
        }
        type SignalsCatalogHandler struct {
            repo    signalsCatalogRepository
            entries []router.Entry           // parsed once at construction
            clock   func() time.Time
        }

D4. Routing wiring
    Replace the deletion-marker comment block at internal/api/router.go:1775-1779
    with two route registrations. Place them just BEFORE the `r.Route("/signals/{vehicleID}", ...)`
    block so the more-specific /signals/catalog path takes precedence over
    /signals/{vehicleID} chi parameter binding. (Chi v5 matches longest
    static-prefix first, but ordering them this way mirrors the
    /vehicle-states/{timeline,summary} precedent and is unambiguous.)

    Same admin-tier rate limit as predecessors: httprate.LimitByIP(60, 1*time.Minute).

D5. ESCAPE HATCH NOT TRIGGERED
    The router package is already imported by other handlers (signals.go,
    fleet_telemetry_handler.go) so calling router.Load() from the new
    handler creates no new layering edge. The //go:embed signals_catalog.json
    fallback is therefore not needed.

    Aggregate query latency: not measurable in this repo (no production
    dataset available). Default-on; if a future operator surfaces >5s
    latency on the hot dataset, drop sample_count + vehicle_count to a
    follow-up prompt. Logged here so the next maintainer sees the
    decision context.

